<Project>

  <!-- Constitution v2.1.0 — Principle III (.NET 10 Modern Standards, NON-NEGOTIABLE) -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>

    <!-- Warnings-as-errors (general C# / nullable diagnostics), all projects -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarningsAsErrors />

    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Native AOT publish (PublishAot) targets OptiPulse.Api — it is the actual
    publish target and the project that matters for startup/footprint
    (Principle III). Build-time AOT/trim ANALYZER enforcement (errors on
    `dotnet build`), however, is scoped only to the true Evaluation Engine
    hot-path projects below, not to Api itself.

    Why not enforce it on Api too: Api is the composition root, and it
    legitimately wires EF Core (Flags/Audit/Identity Infrastructure) — EF Core
    is not fully AOT/trim-compatible as of .NET 10 even in Microsoft's own
    guidance (research.md R5/R13). Turning the analyzer on for Api would fail
    every `dotnet build` on EF's own DbContext constructor and on
    AddDbContext/config-binding calls in Program.cs, none of which are things
    OptiPulse controls or that affect the evaluation hot path. The actual
    zero-allocation, AOT-safe guarantee (Principle II) lives in
    Evaluation.Domain/Application/Infrastructure, which build clean under the
    analyzer with zero EF/reflection usage — that is the real, verified claim.
    A full `dotnet publish -p:PublishAot=true` across the whole graph (which
    would surface Api's genuine EF-related AOT gaps) is a separate, later gate
    (tasks.md T085, Phase 8 Polish) — not required for this MVP build to pass.

    DO NOT re-add `<PublishAot>true</PublishAot>` as a standing property here.
    It is not publish-only: the SDK bakes AOT feature switches into
    OptiPulse.Api.runtimeconfig.json for EVERY build, including Debug. One of
    them is `RuntimeFeature.IsDynamicCodeSupported=false`, which EF Core reads
    to refuse runtime model building entirely:

        System.InvalidOperationException: Model building is not supported when
        publishing with NativeAOT. Use a compiled model.

    That killed `dotnet run` at BootstrapAsync's MigrateAsync — so the host
    could not start at all, which broke the contracts-gen OpenAPI export (the
    CI drift gate boots the real host to dump the spec). The integration tests
    did NOT catch it because they run under the TEST project's runtimeconfig,
    which has no such switch; only Api's own runtimeconfig was affected.

    Turning this on for real requires BOTH of the following first, as T085:
      1. Compiled models (`dotnet ef dbcontext optimize`) for the Flags, Audit,
         and Identity contexts, regenerated whenever the model changes; and
      2. Removing runtime schema work from the startup path — `MigrateAsync`
         needs model building and is not AOT-supported at all, so migrations
         must move to a separate non-AOT tool/job rather than app boot.
    Until both are done, AOT for the host is aspirational, and asserting it via
    this property only breaks the app. Principle III's AOT guarantee is
    meanwhile genuinely enforced where it is verifiable — the Evaluation Engine
    hot path below, which builds clean under the analyzers.
  -->

  <!-- Evaluation Engine hot-path projects: build-time AOT/trim analyzer as a
       hard error, because Principle II's zero-alloc guarantee depends on them
       staying reflection-free. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.StartsWith('OptiPulse.Evaluation'))">
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <EnableAotAnalyzer>true</EnableAotAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
